WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Film Courage: You say the writing process is&nbsp;
about tools and skills not theory, but let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.000
go with theory for one moment. What theories&nbsp;
are stopping a writer before they even start?

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:16.720
Glenn Gers, Screenwriter: That's actually a really&nbsp;
good question because I believe that the theories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.960
are tools. I believe that all the rules I'm not&nbsp;
saying there are no rules there are plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.160
rules there are a lot of rules what there are&nbsp;
not is a single set of rules that always works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.520
and so what you need to do is know every possible&nbsp;
theory you can cram into your head. You should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:39.200
watch all sorts of different types of stories.&nbsp;
You should watch outside-in genres outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.400
the ones you like. You should specifically try to&nbsp;
watch things that are things you don't think you'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.000
be interested in and you should definitely try&nbsp;
to read those screenwriting books or listen to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.880
screenwriting teachers who are teaching very&nbsp;
very different ideas because there are so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:02.560
out there now and the answer is each one of them&nbsp;
will have very valuable parts, very valuable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:10.080
tools that you can use and the only thing that&nbsp;
you need to have is a really big toolbox as big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:16.560
as you can get with every possible theory so that&nbsp;
when you're approaching your work which is really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:23.040
what it's always going to be about you can say I&nbsp;
have seen a story like this or I have seen a story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:30.560
like this but this other story had a different&nbsp;
form for it or this person said I should always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.800
have my transitions in the middle of the story or&nbsp;
this one said I should always have a conflict at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.880
the end of the story. There's so many theories,&nbsp;
none of them is wrong, they’re all right, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:48.240
all there to be used in different circumstances&nbsp;
depending on what you're trying to accomplish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:55.840
The more that you can learn all the different&nbsp;
theories and all the different paradigms and story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:03.520
archetypes that's all good but nothing will help&nbsp;
until you actually sit down and say I am making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:11.600
the choice to write this particular story and this&nbsp;
story needs this set of rules maybe another story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.480
needs another set. Maybe I need to pick one rule&nbsp;
from this one and one rule from that one. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.320
somebody says you know Save The Cat [book&nbsp;
by author Blake Snyder] I happen to believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:28.000
that there are certain parts of Save the Cat the&nbsp;
book that are very valuable, other parts I can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.200
make head or tail of and I don't think that you&nbsp;
should not read it. I think you should read it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:38.720
and take the pieces that you need. I'm not sure&nbsp;
you should alway save the cat but it's good to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:45.520
know that saving the cat is a thing you can do and&nbsp;
I believe that that goes for every screenwriting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:51.360
theory out there and the best thing that you&nbsp;
can do is know all of them so that you can say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:59.440
in this one is this type of character&nbsp;
best revealed by saving a cat or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:03.040
is this one somebody who's going to walk away&nbsp;
and leave the cat there and now we know a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:10.400
different thing about them. How do we still like&nbsp;
them? So really the most important thing is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:18.560
take whatever these rules are and apply them to&nbsp;
your particular goals because that's the thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.680
not every story has the same goals some are trying&nbsp;
to amuse you, some are trying to scare, you some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.480
are trying to make you think they're going to have&nbsp;
very different ways of approaching storytelling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.680
The important thing is that you have as many&nbsp;
tools as you can, as many skills you know how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.960
to make something funny if you need to or how&nbsp;
to make somebody like a character. By the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:44.240
my theory on liking a character is the way&nbsp;
that we like a character is that we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:51.360
what they want. I believe we can really get with&nbsp;
a character who wants something horrible they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:57.600
not saving any cats they're murderers or they're&nbsp;
trying to take over the world and we can still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:04.560
really care about them because we are understand&nbsp;
that they want this thing for their reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.400
and very often that's something that we can care&nbsp;
about even though we don't honestly want them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:16.880
hurt all those people but we get engaged in the&nbsp;
story and we follow the characters because we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:23.840
what they want. My theory so if we don't&nbsp;
know what they what they want then somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.280
do we not trust the character I personally&nbsp;
think we just don't think of them as a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.000
I think if you if you start to pay attention&nbsp;
to how you watch characters I think you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.680
find out that you're always thinking what&nbsp;
do they want what are they trying to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.200
there'll be some form of that you don't just want&nbsp;
to watch someone say oh they have nice clothes I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:47.280
mean you'll watch that for a few minutes but after&nbsp;
a while that person in the cool outfit had better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.960
be trying to do something and people don't try&nbsp;
to do something if they don't want something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.120
because it takes effort to do stuff and we&nbsp;
only do that when we have something we want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.400
if we have that toolbox let's let's&nbsp;
say we put aside the save the cat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:09.840
tool what's one of the most overused whether it's&nbsp;
good or bad tool one or two from this toolbox.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:13.520
We're going to pull it out&nbsp;
and make room for other stuff.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:28.880
I think the three-act structure it's tricky. There&nbsp;
is a world where there's units of entertainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:36.320
which you watch in one dose call them features now&nbsp;
doesn't matter it could be a short but but there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:41.920
a type of story that you watch in one dose and&nbsp;
that does have a three-act structure in the sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:47.280
that it has a beginning middle and end but the&nbsp;
whole set of rules about turning points happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:56.720
at a certain point in a three-act structure is&nbsp;
simply an overused tool it came about because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:01.600
movies were in movie theaters and that was about&nbsp;
as long as a person could sit in a movie theater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:06.320
and also movie theaters wanted to sell a certain&nbsp;
number of tickets so they wanted movies to be of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:11.680
a certain length a lot of this comes down to&nbsp;
business and honestly we shouldn't be making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:17.120
our artistic decisions based on the fact that&nbsp;
movie theaters want to turn over the seats every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:24.080
90 minutes now we have some stories that are&nbsp;
not three acts we have some stories that are 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.920
acts we have some stories that are one act every&nbsp;
story though will have a beginning middle and end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:36.240
so in a way the three act structure will apply&nbsp;
even an episode of a larger thing has a beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:42.640
middle and end I think that's where the three&nbsp;
act thing came from because truthfully even in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:48.320
theater they didn't always have three acts a lot&nbsp;
of plays before the 19th the 20th century had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:54.560
four acts or five acts so three act Save the Cat&nbsp;
possibly doesn't mean they're bad or wrong but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.560 --> 00:07:04.480
it's just they're over used a lot. I'm saying that&nbsp;
you need to choose your form you need to say I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:11.600
going to watch a thousand stories and I'm going to&nbsp;
break some down I strongly suggest that if there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:16.560
something you like watch it like five times watch&nbsp;
it until you're not enjoying it anymore so you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:24.400
start to watch it mechanically and say how do they&nbsp;
do this and when you do that stop like pause and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:29.680
write an outline of the thing you're watching&nbsp;
that you like and learn how it's structured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:33.760
and then do that with a bunch of other things&nbsp;
because you'll find out there's nothing consistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:40.160
the rules are all over the place and furthermore&nbsp;
a lot of these rules are like well you have your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:45.520
inciting incident or your you know beginning twist&nbsp;
or whatever they everybody has names for them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:52.000
none of that really matters because then you&nbsp;
get into an activity which is an academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:58.160
activity of a find the part I'm going to name the&nbsp;
inciting incident what really matters is how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.880
audience feels as they're following the story the&nbsp;
audience is not looking for an inciting incident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:09.520
they're not looking for a second act reversal they&nbsp;
don't care they care about the characters and if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:15.200
you break down the things you love and reverse&nbsp;
engineer them and outline them you'll begin to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:22.400
get a sense of how they do it how many beats does&nbsp;
it take before you are worn out of this thing how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:29.440
long does it go before there's a twist or a&nbsp;
turn the only way you can really become skilled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:38.080
is by breaking down the work and doing it&nbsp;
yourself you know you can study how to build a car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:42.880
until the end of time but you need to&nbsp;
actually put together a car and make it run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:53.840
and you probably have to do that a bunch of&nbsp;
times before you can make a really great car.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:09:00.814
Questions For The Viewers:

00:09:00.814 --> 00:09:02.960
What is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:04.366
